+# Litha speed and reply-integrity candidate
+
+This branch stacks on `79489ad`, the prior isolated Litha candidate. The Forge default branch was freshly fetched and remained `a1eca945`; that repository has no `main` branch. Claude's checkout and branches were not edited. This candidate needs review before integration; it does not change the active STAR engine or sealed evaluation.
+
+## Changes and evidence
+
+1. The chat retains bounded per-view message nodes and changes only rows whose content or terminal state changes. A comparison against the actual prior production module used 100 streamed frames, Home/dock views and 200 synthetic settled turns. Settled formatter calls fell from 3,030 to zero; whole-list replacements fell from 202 to zero; assigned HTML string length fell from 4,268,940 to 78,890 UTF-16 code units. The changing reply is still formatted as a whole. These are inert-DOM operation counts, not browser timing or a model-speed multiplier.
+2. Compact source-capsule JSON and UTF-8 request JSON preserve decoded file data, line numbers, metadata, route/model options and untrusted-source boundaries. Four retained synthetic cases showed 0.79% to 39.35% fewer request bytes. An independent reviewer reproduced them using the actual prior builder and request encoder, including the previous context-size limit. No source line was deleted. Transport savings do not establish model-token savings or inference latency.
+3. A normal `stop` terminal closes the stream without reading again. `length` produces a typed incomplete terminal after yielding any validated final text; the UI retains partial code with an escaped warning and excludes that assistant fragment from successful history. Unsupported and unknown finish reasons fail visibly. No automatic continuation request is sent.
+4. Both raw response bytes and decoded JSON text must be valid UTF-8. This prevents a provider's escaped lone surrogate from silently corrupting code. Invalid request Unicode fails before reading the engine key. Authentication headers, no-redirect handling, default prompts, model preferences, generation limits, sampling and thinking options are unchanged.
+
+## Verification
+
+Existing tests and evaluation fixtures are unchanged. New retained checks cover completion integrity (7 groups), context wire equivalence (2 groups), and actual incremental rendering (129 checks, or 132 with an optional trusted baseline module). Earlier protocol, context, session, app-hook, chat and portrait checks remain in the build pipeline. Independent reviewers reran the focused checks and inspected the final source.
+
+Root also used a real browser DOM with a byte-identical copy of the production chat module and synthetic bridge events. Observed: Home retained four rows and Dock 60; settled node identity survived chunks; draft text remained; an older reading position stayed at scrollTop 3501 while scrollHeight grew from 4749 to 4769; incomplete text and the escaped warning remained visible. The actual composer, Stop control and normal-completion path were exercised, and a new question followed the bottom. This isolated fixture did not load a model, use credentials or read project files. Evidence screenshot is local at `build/litha-speed-browser.png` and is excluded from the package/source commit.
+
+The first-start guide and local Windows package are refreshed. `dist/BUILD_RECEIPT.json` identifies final source, checked frozen modules, web resources, artifact hashes and ZIP integrity. Native Qt installation/connected-engine performance, coding accuracy and GPU latency remain unverified. The source changes are practical optimizations and correctness repairs; no claim of unprecedented intelligence or a new foundation model is made.
+
+Research links and rejected broader changes are in `docs/research/litha-speed-quality-20261028.md`. Larger token budgets, different models, moving source for prefix caching, general SSE framing and autonomous editing need their own evidence and review. No active model evaluation, account, release, updater activation or permissions were changed.
